# MASTER BRIEF — "Stablecoin flows and T-bill yields after the GENIUS Act"

## 0. Who you are and what this is
You are acting as two people at once: (a) a senior empirical macro-finance researcher in the style of a BIS/Fed economist, and (b) a senior Python data engineer. I am an independent researcher. The output is a short, reproducible empirical note that I will post on LinkedIn, aimed at BIS researchers (the authors of the anchor paper below are the target audience). Hard constraint: everything — data, estimation, figures, write-up — must be finished in **12 hours wall-clock** from now. Quality bar: "a BIS economist reads it and thinks: careful, honest, replicable, worth a comment", not "a crypto blog post".

Effort allocation (I planned this; follow it):
- **40% academic**: aligning with the anchor paper, pre-analysis plan, identification logic, interpretation, limitations, writing the note and the post.
- **60% technical**: data pipeline, estimation code, tests, figures, repo hygiene.

MODE: `checkpoint` — stop at the 3 checkpoints marked ⏸ and wait for me to type `continue`. If I write `MODE: autonomous` at any point, run end-to-end without stopping.

Environment: Windows 11 + WSL2 (Ubuntu), 32 GB RAM, i5-13500, RTX 4070 Ti. **Do not use the GPU**; this is a CPU/pandas project. Python 3.11+. Internet access is available for data downloads and web lookups.

## 1. Research question (do not expand scope)
Primary: **Did the response of US Treasury-bill yields to stablecoin flows change after the GENIUS Act was signed into law (18 July 2025)?**

Secondary (only these two):
1. Maturity shift: given the Act's reserve rule (T-bills with ≤93 days remaining maturity), did the response concentrate at the short end (4-week / 3-month) relative to 6-month, 1-year and 2-year?
2. Asymmetry: is the outflow effect still larger than the inflow effect post-enactment?

Anchor paper: Ahmed, R. and I. Aldasoro (2025), "Stablecoins and safe asset prices", BIS Working Paper No 1270, May 2025 — likely at https://www.bis.org/publ/work1270.pdf [VERIFY number, URL and details on the web before quoting]. My recollection of their headline [VERIFY from the PDF]: a 5-day stablecoin inflow of ~USD 3.5 bn lowers 3-month T-bill yields by ~2–2.5 bp within 10 days; outflows raise yields 2–3× more. Frame our work explicitly as **"replication-in-spirit + extension"** of this paper, not as a new causal claim. In Phase 0, fetch the paper and record its exact specification, sample, controls and headline numbers in docs/FINDINGS.md so our spec can be aligned where feasible.

Hypotheses (write into docs/PAP.md BEFORE running any regression):
- H1: |β_h^post| > |β_h^pre| for 3-month bills at h ≈ 10 (stronger pass-through after the Act).
- H2: post-enactment, the response at 4-week / 3-month exceeds that at 6-month and longer; pre-enactment the term pattern is flatter.
- H3: asymmetry (outflows > inflows) persists in both regimes.
A null result on any hypothesis is a legitimate, publishable finding. Do not torture the data.

Institutional caveat to carry through all writing: the Act's reserve requirements are **not yet binding** — they take effect at the earlier of 18 months after enactment (~Jan 2027) or 120 days after final regulations [VERIFY]. So "post" means a post-enactment / anticipation regime, not a compliance regime. Say this plainly.

## 2. Data (all free, no API keys)
Build fetchers in `src/data/` with on-disk caching (raw JSON/CSV under `data/raw/` with a fetch timestamp in the filename). Never fabricate or hand-edit data. If a source fails for more than 20 minutes, switch to the listed fallback and log it in FINDINGS.md.

A. Stablecoin circulating supply (daily) — DefiLlama
- List + ids: https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoins?includePrices=true
- History per coin: https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoincharts/all?stablecoin={id} (fields such as `date` in unix seconds and `totalCirculating.peggedUSD` — inspect the JSON and adapt; look ids up by symbol, never hardcode).
- Primary universe: fiat-backed USD payment stablecoins — USDT, USDC, FDUSD, PYUSD, TUSD, USDP, BUSD (defunct, keep history), RLUSD, plus any other clearly fiat/T-bill-backed coin ≥ USD 1 bn. **Exclude** crypto-collateralised / synthetic coins (DAI/USDS, USDe, FRAX, etc.) from the primary aggregate; keep them in a secondary "all USD-pegged" aggregate for robustness.
- Sample: earliest available to latest. Report the actual start date.
- Timing: a DefiLlama snapshot dated d is taken at 00:00 UTC; treat it as the value at the **end of calendar day d−1** (US time). Document this. Robustness: shift by one day.
- Fallback: CoinGecko https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/coins/{tether|usd-coin|...}/market_chart?vs_currency=usd&days=max → `market_caps` (respect rate limits; sleep between calls).

B. Yields and controls (daily) — FRED CSV, no key
Pattern: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id={SERIES} (treat "." as NaN)
- Outcomes: DTB4WK (4-week bill), **DTB3 (3-month, primary)**, DTB6 (6-month), DGS1 (1-year), DGS2 (2-year; placebo and expectations proxy). Also DGS1MO, DGS3MO as constant-maturity alternatives.
- Controls: EFFR, IORB, RRPONTSYD (ON RRP, USD bn), VIXCLS, SP500, DTWEXBGS (broad dollar index).
- Fallback for bills: US Treasury daily bill rates CSV, pattern https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/daily-treasury-rates.csv/{YEAR}/all?type=daily_treasury_bill_rates&field_tdr_date_value={YEAR}&_format=csv (loop over years; adapt if the pattern changed).

C. Crypto risk control (daily)
- BTC close: Binance https://api.binance.com/api/v3/klines?symbol=BTCUSDT&interval=1d&startTime={ms}&limit=1000 (paginate). Fallback: CoinGecko bitcoin market_chart.

D. Event dates (hardcode in config.yaml with a source comment; VERIFY each)
- 2022-05-09 Terra/UST collapse begins; 2022-11-08 FTX; 2023-03-10 SVB failure / USDC depeg; 2025-04-02 US tariff announcement; 2025-06-17 Senate passes GENIUS; 2025-07-17 House passes; **2025-07-18 signed into law (primary break date)**.

E. Optional — only if Phases 1–3 finish early: net T-bill issuance from TreasuryDirect auctions https://www.treasurydirect.gov/TA_WS/securities/search?format=json&type=Bill as a supply control.

Units: supply in USD bn; yields converted to **basis points**; returns in percent.

## 3. Pre-analysis plan (write docs/PAP.md FIRST, commit it, then estimate)
Primary specification — Jordà (2005) local projections on US business days t, horizons h = 0, …, 20:

  y_{t+h} − y_{t−1} = α_h + β_h·F_t + δ_h·(F_t × Post_t) + γ_h·Post_t + Γ_h′·X_t + ε_{t+h}

- y: DTB3 in bp. F_t: 5-business-day change in the primary aggregate supply, USD bn (F_t = S_t − S_{t−5} on the business-day calendar, so weekend changes accrue to Monday). Post_t = 1{t ≥ 2025-07-18}.
- X_t baseline: lags 1–5 of Δy and of daily flow; contemporaneous ΔDGS2_t (policy-expectations proxy), ΔVIX_t, BTC return_t, S&P return_t, Δlog(RRP)_t. Robustness: lags only, no contemporaneous controls — report both and discuss the "bad control" trade-off in one paragraph.
- Inference: Newey–West HAC with maxlags = h+1. Report 68% and 90% bands.
- Report β_h (pre), β_h + δ_h (post), δ_h with p-values, N_pre and N_post.
- Scaling for readers: per USD 10 bn flow and per 1 s.d. of F; one table row rescaled to USD 3.5 bn to compare with the anchor paper.

Secondary specs (pre-registered): (i) same model for DTB4WK, DTB6, DGS1, DGS2 → "term structure of β_10"; (ii) asymmetry with F⁺ = max(F,0), F⁻ = min(F,0), each interacted with Post; (iii) spread outcome DTB3 − EFFR.

Robustness (pre-registered; run only these): alternative break 2025-06-17; placebo break 2024-07-18; drop ±10 business days around Terra, FTX and SVB; USDT-only and USDC-only flows; all-USD-pegged aggregate; 1-day timing shift; flow windows k = 1 and k = 10.

Rule: anything not in the PAP that you run later is labelled "post hoc" in FINDINGS.md and in the note. Never change the primary spec after seeing results.

## 4. Technical requirements
Repo layout (create exactly this; no notebooks required):

  stablecoin-tbills-genius-act/
    README.md                # the 2-page research note (see §6)
    Makefile                 # `make all` = fetch → build → estimate → figures → tables
    requirements.txt         # pandas numpy statsmodels matplotlib requests pyyaml pyarrow pytest
    config.yaml              # series ids, universe, dates, horizons, break dates
    src/
      data/fetch_defillama.py  fetch_fred.py  fetch_crypto.py  build_panel.py
      analysis/lp.py  robustness.py
      viz/figures.py  tables.py
      pipeline.py            # orchestrates everything; idempotent
    tests/test_data_sanity.py
    data/raw/  data/processed/     # commit processed parquet + small raw files (<50 MB total)
    output/figures/  output/tables/
    docs/PAP.md  FINDINGS.md  LINKEDIN.md  SUMMARY_TH.md  REPO_META.md

Standards: Python 3.11+ in a venv (`python3 -m venv .venv`), type hints, small pure functions, docstrings, `logging` not `print` for pipeline steps, deterministic (fixed seed if anything is bootstrapped), ruff-clean if available. No classes unless they simplify. No Docker, no dashboards, no ML, no GPU libraries.

Data-sanity tests (pytest) must pass before any regression: no duplicate dates; monotone dates; latest observation ≤ 7 days old; supply > 0 for every coin; yields within 0–7%; share of NaNs after alignment < 2% and logged; N_pre and N_post printed. If you can look up one web-reported supply figure per major coin, assert our series is within ±30% of it; otherwise skip and log.

Figures (matplotlib, 300 dpi PNG + SVG, one consistent style, Source/Note footer on every figure):
- fig1_irf_pre_post (the hero): β_h pre (grey) vs post (colour) for DTB3 overlaid, 90% and 68% bands, h = 0–20, scaled per USD 10 bn; subtitle with N_pre/N_post and sample dates.
- fig2_term_structure: β_10 with 90% CI across 4w, 3m, 6m, 1y, 2y, pre vs post side by side.
- fig3_data_overview: top panel stacked supply by coin (USD bn); bottom panel DTB4WK and DTB3 with vertical lines at the event dates.
- fig4_asymmetry: inflow vs outflow IRFs, pre vs post (2×2 small multiples).
- fig_linkedin: fig1 re-rendered at 1200×1200 px, large fonts, one-sentence takeaway as the title.
Tables: table1_primary (.md + .csv): β_h at h = 0, 5, 10, 20 for pre / post / difference with SEs and N. table2_robustness (.md): one row per robustness check with β_10 pre / post / diff.

Git: `git init`, commit at the end of every phase with a clear message. Do not push; I will.

Repo metadata: the GitHub repo is `stablecoin-tbills-genius-act`. Write docs/REPO_META.md containing (a) the one-line "About" description: "Do stablecoin flows move US T-bill yields differently after the GENIUS Act? Replication-in-spirit + extension of Ahmed & Aldasoro (2025, BIS WP). Open data, local projections, fully reproducible." and (b) GitHub topics: stablecoins, treasury-bills, genius-act, monetary-policy, local-projections, bis, open-data, python. Use the URL placeholder https://github.com/<my-handle>/stablecoin-tbills-genius-act everywhere a repo link is needed; I will replace <my-handle>.

